Description: Quick: draw a frame made for an expose as a pair too
 A window whose elements show depth is drawn once per eye, but a frame
 drawn for an expose was drawn once and put into both eye targets, and
 a still window is not drawn again. So a window that stayed still after
 an expose kept one picture in both eyes: on Wayland after a frame
 callback that came later than QtWayland's limit, after the window was
 hidden, minimized or on another desktop. The Stereo Demonstrator's quad
 buffer route showed a flat scene on a side by side (full) output in a
 guest that draws in software, where every late frame ends in an expose.
 .
 The threaded render loop now draws an expose's frame as a pair: the
 left eye is synchronized as in any frame, and the gui thread waits, as
 an expose does, until the right eye is rendered and both are presented.
 Asking for one more frame after the expose instead does not settle
 where that frame's callback is late again: the picture then alternates.
 The basic render loop draws the pair in every frame and needs nothing.
 .
 tst_qquickwindowstereo::drawsThePairAfterAnExpose() exposes a still
 window and expects every frame presented from then on to hold both
 eyes.
